WEBVTT
NOTE how-are-microchips-designed - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.628
Microchips are designed mostly as text.

00:00:02.628 --> 00:00:08.019
Engineers describe a chip's behaviour in a
hardware description language such as

00:00:08.019 --> 00:00:12.938
Verilog or VHDL, and software tools
translate that description into logic

00:00:12.938 --> 00:00:17.520
gates and then into the geometric layout
of billions of transistors.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:21.099
Both languages are published as standards
by the IEEE.

00:00:21.099 --> 00:00:24.811
So the answer to who draws the chip is,
largely, nobody.

00:00:24.811 --> 00:00:26.361
It is not drawn by hand.

00:00:26.361 --> 00:00:31.141
What that answer does not settle is how
anyone knows the result will work.

00:00:31.141 --> 00:00:35.909
Nobody can inspect billions of transistors
before the factory runs, and a mistake

00:00:35.909 --> 00:00:37.675
found afterwards is expensive.

00:00:37.675 --> 00:00:42.565
The real story of chip design is the story
of how that confidence is built.

00:00:42.565 --> 00:00:46.603
Start with the language, because that is
where the design actually lives.

00:00:46.603 --> 00:00:51.154
A hardware description language looks like
code, and engineers type it in an editor

00:00:51.154 --> 00:00:52.251
like any programmer.

00:00:52.251 --> 00:00:56.000
But it does not describe steps to be run
one after another.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.118
It describes structure and behaviour that
exist all at once: these signals connect

00:01:01.118 --> 00:01:06.299
here, this value updates on every tick of
the clock, this block waits for that one.

00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:11.887
The two dominant languages, Verilog and
VHDL, along with an extended form called

00:01:11.887 --> 00:01:15.659
SystemVerilog, are published as standards
by the IEEE.

00:01:15.659 --> 00:01:21.476
That is how every tool vendor and every
design team can agree on what a given line

00:01:21.476 --> 00:01:22.469
of text means.

00:01:22.469 --> 00:01:24.074
The agreement is not a detail.

00:01:24.074 --> 00:01:28.191
Without a shared, written definition of
the language, a design could mean one

00:01:28.191 --> 00:01:32.416
thing in the simulator and something
slightly different in the tool that builds

00:01:32.416 --> 00:01:32.576
it.

00:01:32.576 --> 00:01:37.801
Most designs are written at what engineers
call the register-transfer level.

00:01:37.801 --> 00:01:41.032
A register is a small store that holds a
value.

00:01:41.032 --> 00:01:45.432
The description says how values move
between registers, and what logic

00:01:45.432 --> 00:01:48.827
transforms them on the way, at each edge
of the clock.

00:01:48.827 --> 00:01:54.208
Nothing at this level says where a
transistor sits or how long a wire is.

00:01:54.208 --> 00:01:56.068
That separation is deliberate.

00:01:56.068 --> 00:02:01.215
It lets a team reason about what the chip
should compute before anyone decides what

00:02:01.215 --> 00:02:05.866
it physically looks like, and it lets the
same description be rebuilt for a

00:02:05.866 --> 00:02:07.355
different factory later.

00:02:07.355 --> 00:02:11.659
The next step is called logic synthesis,
and it is done by software.

00:02:11.659 --> 00:02:16.128
The synthesis tool reads the
register-transfer description and maps it

00:02:16.128 --> 00:02:18.235
onto a library of standard cells.

00:02:18.235 --> 00:02:23.386
A standard cell is a small, pre-designed
circuit — a gate that performs AND, a gate

00:02:23.386 --> 00:02:27.793
that inverts a signal, a storage element
called a flip-flop — drawn and

00:02:27.793 --> 00:02:31.765
characterised in advance for one specific
manufacturing process.

00:02:31.765 --> 00:02:35.716
The factory, usually called a foundry, or
a library vendor working with it, has

00:02:35.716 --> 00:02:39.716
measured how fast each cell switches and
how much power it draws under different

00:02:39.716 --> 00:02:41.616
conditions of voltage and temperature.

00:02:41.616 --> 00:02:46.480
The output of synthesis is a netlist: a
list of cells and the wires that connect

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:46.784
them.

00:02:46.784 --> 00:02:50.860
Here is the first place the "how we know"
question bites.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:56.581
Synthesis does not simply translate; it
optimises, rewriting logic to be smaller

00:02:56.581 --> 00:02:57.296
or faster.

00:02:57.296 --> 00:03:01.333
So the netlist is not a line-by-line copy
of what the engineer wrote.

00:03:01.333 --> 00:03:06.204
To confirm the two still mean the same
thing, teams run equivalence checking, a

00:03:06.204 --> 00:03:10.767
formal method that proves mathematically
that the netlist and the original

00:03:10.767 --> 00:03:14.096
description produce identical outputs for
every input.

00:03:14.096 --> 00:03:16.504
It is not a test that tries examples.

00:03:16.504 --> 00:03:21.581
It is a proof for every input, and that
difference matters for everything that

00:03:21.581 --> 00:03:22.101
follows.

00:03:22.101 --> 00:03:27.116
Deep-sea science builds conclusions from
incomplete observations, damaged

00:03:27.116 --> 00:03:31.237
specimens, sonar, water samples and tiny
windows of footage.

00:03:31.237 --> 00:03:36.597
That question, asked about animals, is
exactly the right one to ask about chips.

00:03:36.597 --> 00:03:41.888
A modern processor has far more possible
internal states than any simulation could

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:42.597
ever visit.

00:03:42.597 --> 00:03:45.462
So no one observes a chip's behaviour
exhaustively.

00:03:45.462 --> 00:03:49.842
Verification engineers build a testbench,
a surrounding program that feeds the

00:03:49.842 --> 00:03:54.167
design inputs and checks its outputs
against a reference model of what should

00:03:54.167 --> 00:03:54.560
happen.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:59.349
On many teams verification takes more
effort than writing the design itself,

00:03:59.349 --> 00:04:04.389
though published estimates of the split
vary and this script will not quote one.

00:04:04.389 --> 00:04:09.605
Engineers generate constrained random
stimulus, enormous numbers of plausible

00:04:09.605 --> 00:04:14.955
but unplanned input sequences, because
bugs hide in combinations no one thought

00:04:14.955 --> 00:04:16.107
to write by hand.

00:04:16.107 --> 00:04:21.151
Then they measure coverage: which lines
executed, which states were reached, which

00:04:21.151 --> 00:04:23.304
scenarios the plan said must occur.

00:04:23.304 --> 00:04:25.239
Coverage is the honest part.

00:04:25.239 --> 00:04:30.147
It tells the team what was exercised and,
by subtraction, what was not.

00:04:30.147 --> 00:04:34.210
Where simulation cannot reach, formal
verification steps in.

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:39.628
A tool is given a property — this buffer
never overflows, these two requests are

00:04:39.628 --> 00:04:44.979
never granted at once — and searches for
any sequence of inputs that breaks it.

00:04:44.979 --> 00:04:50.349
If none exists, the property is proven
within the bounds the tool was given.

00:04:50.349 --> 00:04:55.231
For large designs, teams also load the
description onto emulation hardware, or

00:04:55.231 --> 00:04:59.926
onto reconfigurable chips called FPGAs,
which run the logic far faster than

00:04:59.926 --> 00:05:05.246
simulation and let real software boot on a
design that does not physically exist yet.

00:05:05.246 --> 00:05:07.875
Each method covers a gap the others leave.

00:05:07.875 --> 00:05:10.129
Only now does the chip acquire a shape.

00:05:10.129 --> 00:05:14.636
Physical design begins with floorplanning:
deciding where large blocks such as

00:05:14.636 --> 00:05:18.451
memories and processor cores will sit, and
how power reaches them.

00:05:18.451 --> 00:05:22.453
Placement software then positions every
standard cell from the netlist.

00:05:22.453 --> 00:05:27.020
Clock tree synthesis builds the network
that delivers the clock signal so that it

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:30.008
arrives at every flip-flop at nearly the
same moment.

00:05:30.008 --> 00:05:35.532
Routing draws the metal wires that connect
everything, stacked in many layers above

00:05:35.532 --> 00:05:36.597
the transistors.

00:05:36.597 --> 00:05:41.210
All of this is automated, because the
number of cells is far too large for

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:46.384
hands, but engineers steer it constantly,
adjusting constraints and re-running when

00:05:46.384 --> 00:05:47.568
results fall short.

00:05:47.568 --> 00:05:52.779
How do they know when results fall short?
Mainly through static timing analysis.

00:05:52.779 --> 00:05:58.173
Instead of simulating signals, the tool
adds up the delay along every path between

00:05:58.173 --> 00:06:03.239
registers, using the characterised cell
data and estimates of wire delay, and

00:06:03.239 --> 00:06:07.120
checks that each signal arrives before the
next clock edge.

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:11.033
A path that is too slow is called a timing
violation.

00:06:11.033 --> 00:06:16.791
Static timing analysis is powerful because
it is exhaustive over paths without

00:06:16.791 --> 00:06:18.784
needing any input patterns.

00:06:18.784 --> 00:06:24.057
Its boundary is that it is only as good as
the delay models it is fed, which is why

00:06:24.057 --> 00:06:28.187
the careful measurement work done by the
foundry matters so much.

00:06:28.187 --> 00:06:31.491
Before a design leaves the team, it goes
through signoff.

00:06:31.491 --> 00:06:36.245
Design rule checking compares every shape
in the layout against the foundry's rule

00:06:36.245 --> 00:06:39.840
set: minimum widths, minimum spacings, how
layers may overlap.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:44.289
Those rules encode what the manufacturing
process can reliably print.

00:06:44.289 --> 00:06:49.447
Layout versus schematic checking extracts
the circuit that the geometry actually

00:06:49.447 --> 00:06:52.155
forms and confirms it matches the netlist.

00:06:52.155 --> 00:06:56.937
Parasitic extraction calculates the
resistance and capacitance of the real

00:06:56.937 --> 00:07:00.363
wires, and timing is checked again with
those values.

00:07:00.363 --> 00:07:05.477
A design that passes all of this is handed
off in a step still called tape-out, a

00:07:05.477 --> 00:07:10.213
name commonly traced to the era when
layouts were shipped on magnetic tape.

00:07:10.213 --> 00:07:15.276
The layout becomes a set of photomasks,
the stencils used to pattern each layer of

00:07:15.276 --> 00:07:15.893
the wafer.

00:07:15.893 --> 00:07:19.442
Here the design world meets the public
standards bodies.

00:07:19.442 --> 00:07:24.322
SEMI, the industry association for
semiconductor manufacturing, publishes the

00:07:24.322 --> 00:07:29.518
SEMI International Standards used across
equipment, materials and masks, so that a

00:07:29.518 --> 00:07:33.637
design from one company can be built with
tools from many others.

00:07:33.637 --> 00:07:37.784
NIST, the United States national
measurement institute, works on

00:07:37.784 --> 00:07:42.902
semiconductor metrology — how to measure
features far too small to see, and how

00:07:42.902 --> 00:07:45.429
sure anyone can be of that measurement.

00:07:45.429 --> 00:07:47.220
These bodies do not design chips.

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:51.560
They supply the shared definitions and
measurements that let a design be trusted

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:53.296
outside the room it was made in.

00:07:53.296 --> 00:07:55.658
Everything up to tape-out is prediction.

00:07:55.658 --> 00:07:59.968
The first wafers back from the foundry are
where prediction meets matter.

00:07:59.968 --> 00:08:04.613
Engineers run bring-up: they power the
first chips, check that clocks start, load

00:08:04.613 --> 00:08:07.824
test software, and compare behaviour
against the models.

00:08:07.824 --> 00:08:13.056
To make this possible, designers add
circuitry whose only purpose is to be

00:08:13.056 --> 00:08:17.943
Scan chains link the flip-flops so their
contents can be shifted out and read,

00:08:17.943 --> 00:08:22.768
which lets automated test equipment check
each manufactured chip for physical

00:08:22.768 --> 00:08:23.269
defects.

00:08:23.269 --> 00:08:28.230
This practice is called design for test,
and it is why a chip can be checked after

00:08:28.230 --> 00:08:29.984
manufacture, not only before.

00:08:29.984 --> 00:08:34.232
When bring-up finds a bug that slipped
through, the outcome depends on where it

00:08:34.232 --> 00:08:34.555
lives.

00:08:34.555 --> 00:08:38.073
Some bugs can be worked around in software
or firmware.

00:08:38.073 --> 00:08:43.062
Others require a respin: corrected masks
and another trip through the factory,

00:08:43.062 --> 00:08:44.789
which costs time and money.

00:08:44.789 --> 00:08:50.612
Manufacturers also publish lists of known
bugs in shipped products, usually called

00:08:50.612 --> 00:08:51.109
errata.

00:08:51.109 --> 00:08:55.905
Those documents are some of the most
candid public evidence about chip design,

00:08:55.905 --> 00:09:00.456
because they show directly that
verification reduces risk without removing

00:09:00.456 --> 00:09:00.640
it.

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:04.485
In the edit, watch for the shift between
two kinds of evidence.

00:09:04.485 --> 00:09:09.988
Some steps, like equivalence checking,
formal proofs and static timing analysis,

00:09:09.988 --> 00:09:12.533
are exhaustive within a stated model.

00:09:12.533 --> 00:09:16.076
Others, like simulation and bring-up, are
samples.

00:09:16.076 --> 00:09:18.768
The animation draws those differently.

00:09:18.768 --> 00:09:23.467
A proven claim is drawn solid; a sampled
one is drawn as scattered points with gaps

00:09:23.467 --> 00:09:24.203
between them.

00:09:24.203 --> 00:09:29.214
Whenever you see the gaps, that is the
honest picture of what testing alone can

00:09:29.214 --> 00:09:29.531
show.

00:09:29.531 --> 00:09:34.405
This account describes the method as
public standards and teaching material lay

00:09:34.405 --> 00:09:34.837
it out.

00:09:34.837 --> 00:09:39.087
It does not establish how any particular
company runs its flow.

00:09:39.087 --> 00:09:44.350
Those details, including the tools chosen,
the size of teams and the cost of a

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:49.409
design, are mostly proprietary, and
published estimates vary so widely that

00:09:49.409 --> 00:09:51.568
this script states none of them.

00:09:51.568 --> 00:09:55.819
It also does not establish that any
shipped chip is free of bugs.

00:09:55.819 --> 00:10:00.447
Verification proves properties inside
models, and the models themselves are

00:10:00.447 --> 00:10:02.792
assumptions about how silicon behaves.

00:10:02.792 --> 00:10:06.619
The published errata are the reminder of
where that gap shows.

00:10:06.619 --> 00:10:11.612
So a microchip is designed the way a
careful argument is written: stated in a

00:10:11.612 --> 00:10:17.125
precise language, translated step by step,
and checked at each step against something

00:10:17.125 --> 00:10:18.357
it must agree with.

00:10:18.357 --> 00:10:21.979
The chip you are holding is the last link
in that chain.

00:10:21.979 --> 00:10:26.816
The evidence that it works is not one
test, but the agreement of all of them.
